  <!-- H1 -->
  <h1 class="page-h1">Florida Quitclaim Deed for Divorce: What the Decree Doesn't Do</h1>

  <p class="last-updated">Updated August 7, 2026 using the primary authorities cited below.</p>

  <!-- Callout at top &mdash; key distinction -->
  <Callout type="info" title="A deed changes title. It does not remove a borrower from the note or mortgage.">
    <p>
      Title: who owns the property. Note: who owes the debt. Mortgage: the lien/security instrument.
      These are three separate legal items. A quitclaim deed addresses only the first.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- Intro -->
  <p>
    When a Florida divorce ends with one spouse keeping the family home, most people assume the divorce
    decree takes care of everything. It doesn't. The decree orders the transfer &mdash; it doesn't execute it.
    If no deed or other legally effective instrument changes record title, the public land records may
    continue to show both spouses as owners, regardless of what the court said.
  </p>
  <p>
    This distinction causes real problems: clouds on title, refinancing roadblocks, and complications
    when the property eventually sells. A Florida quitclaim deed is the instrument that actually moves
    ownership from one spouse to the other, and it has to be prepared, signed, and recorded correctly
    to do its job.
  </p>

  <!-- Why Quitclaim Not Warranty -->
  <h2>Why a Quitclaim Deed (Not a Warranty Deed) for Divorce</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>warranty deed</strong> carries title guarantees: the grantor promises the title is clean,
    free of undisclosed encumbrances, and that they will defend the grantee against any future claims.
    That is appropriate between strangers in a typical arm's-length sale.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>quitclaim deed</strong> makes no such promise. The grantor conveys whatever interest they
    actually hold &mdash; nothing more, nothing less. The transferring spouse is not warranting the title
    history of the home; they are simply releasing their ownership interest to their former spouse.
    For divorce property transfers in Florida, a quitclaim deed is legally appropriate and widely used
    in divorce-related chains of title.
  </p>

  <!-- Decree vs Deed -->
  <h2>The Decree vs. Deed Distinction: The Most Commonly Missed Step</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>divorce decree</strong> (or final judgment of dissolution) is a court order. It obligates
    each party to carry out its terms, including transferring property. It creates a legal duty. What it
    does not do is actually change title.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>quitclaim deed</strong> is a conveyance instrument used to transfer the grantor's ownership
    interest. Recording places the conveyance in the public land records and affects third-party rights
    and priority. This is the step that changes who owns the property in the public record.
  </p>
  <p>
    Many divorcing couples treat the decree as sufficient and never get around to preparing and recording
    the deed. Years later, when the spouse who kept the home tries to sell or refinance, the title search
    reveals that the ex-spouse's name is still on the deed. The solution is straightforward: after the
    divorce is final, prepare the quitclaim deed, execute it according to Florida's requirements, and
    record it with the county clerk. Don't wait.
  </p>

  <!-- DeedForm CTA &mdash; after Decree vs Deed section -->
