    <h2 class="cta-block__heading">Generate Your Florida Divorce Quitclaim Deed</h2>
    <p class="cta-block__body">
      Florida-specific form already formatted to meet Florida's statutory requirements &mdash; two-witness
      blocks, notary acknowledgment, and correct consideration language for divorce transfers.
    </p>
    <DeedForm />
  </div>

  <Disclaimer />

  <!-- Mortgage Warning -->
  <h2>The Mortgage Problem: What the Deed Doesn't Fix</h2>
  <Callout type="warning" title="Critical: the deed removes you from title &mdash; not from the mortgage">
    <p>
      A quitclaim deed transfers <strong>title</strong> &mdash; the ownership interest in the property. It
      does not touch the <strong>mortgage</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If both spouses signed the original mortgage note, both remain liable to the lender after the deed
      is recorded. The lender was not a party to the divorce. The divorce decree dividing the property
      does not change the loan contract. The bank can still pursue the departing spouse for the debt if
      the remaining spouse stops making payments, defaults, or goes through foreclosure.
    </p>
    <p>
      The only ways to remove someone from a mortgage are: <strong>refinancing</strong> (the remaining
      spouse takes out a new loan in their name alone) or <strong>loan assumption</strong> (lender formally
      approves a change in borrowers &mdash; less common with conventional loans). Neither happens automatically.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    If a divorce decree requires one spouse to remove the other from loan liability, a quitclaim deed
    alone does not accomplish that. Resolution typically requires a lender-approved assumption, refinance,
    payoff or sale, or another lender-approved arrangement.
  </p>

  <!-- Who Signs -->
  <h2>Who Signs the Quitclaim Deed</h2>
  <p>
    Only the <strong>grantor</strong> &mdash; the spouse transferring their interest &mdash; needs to sign the
    quitclaim deed. The grantee (the spouse receiving the property) does not sign, though both names
    appear in the deed.
  </p>
  <p>The grantor's signature must be:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Witnessed by <strong>two subscribing witnesses</strong> (Fla. Stat. §689.01)</li>
    <li>Acknowledged before a <strong>notary public</strong> (Fla. Stat. §695.03)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The grantor signs in the presence of two subscribing witnesses. They sign in the designated witness
    lines on the deed form, and the notary completes the acknowledgment block confirming that the grantor
    appeared before them and acknowledged signing the instrument.
  </p>

  <!-- Documentary Stamp Tax -->
  <h2>Documentary Stamp Tax</h2>
  <p>
    Florida's Documentary Stamp Tax (Fla. Stat. § 201.02) normally applies to deed transfers. For most
    transfers, the rate is $0.70 per $100 of consideration.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Transfer of the marital home after divorce:</strong> Florida provides a documentary-stamp
    exemption for qualifying transfers of the marital home or an interest in it between spouses or former
    spouses following dissolution of marriage. Confirm that the specific property and transfer fall within
    the exemption with a tax professional or the Florida DOR before claiming it. Retain documentation
    supporting the exemption and follow current DOR and county requirements.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Other real estate transferred in a divorce:</strong> For real estate that was not the marital
    home, the documentary-stamp treatment depends on the consideration involved, including mortgages,
    liens, or other encumbrances. Do not assume the divorce exemption applies automatically.
  </p>

  <!-- What If You Don't Record -->
  <h2>What Happens If You Don't Record the Deed</h2>
  <p>Failing to record a deed after a Florida divorce carries risks that compound over time:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Title remains in both names.</strong> Future creditors, lienholders, or purchasers searching title will see the prior spouse's interest as unresolved.</li>
    <li><strong>Cloud on title.</strong> When the property is eventually sold, title insurance will identify the missing deed as a defect. Clearing it requires locating the ex-spouse &mdash; who may have remarried, moved, or become difficult to reach.</li>
    <li><strong>Refinancing obstacles.</strong> Most lenders require clean title in the borrower's name before approving a refinance.</li>
    <li><strong>Probate complications.</strong> If either party dies before the deed is recorded, their interest in the property may pass through their estate, creating additional legal proceedings.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Step-by-Step -->
  <h2>Step-by-Step: Transferring Property with a Quitclaim Deed in a Florida Divorce</h2>
  <div class="process-steps">
    <ProcessStep
      number={1}
      title="Draft the deed"
      description="Use a Florida-specific quitclaim deed form that includes the full legal description of the property. The legal description comes from the county property appraiser's records &mdash; typically found on the existing deed."
    />
    <ProcessStep
      number={2}
      title="Fill in the required information"
      description="Complete the grantor's name and address, the grantee's name and address, and the legal description. Retain documentation supporting any divorce-related documentary-stamp exemption and follow current DOR and county requirements."
    />
    <ProcessStep
      number={3}
      title="Sign with two witnesses and a notary"
      description="Arrange for two subscribing witnesses. The grantor signs the deed in the presence of the two witnesses and the notary. The notary completes the acknowledgment block and affixes their seal."
    />
    <ProcessStep
      number={4}
      title="Record the deed with the county clerk"
      description="Submit the original executed deed to the clerk of the circuit court in the county where the property is located, along with the recording fee. Recording fees vary by county."
    />
    <ProcessStep
      number={5}
      title="Notify the mortgage lender in writing"
      description="Send the lender a copy of the recorded deed and a written notice of the ownership change. The deed does not affect the loan obligation, but keeping the lender informed creates a paper trail."
    />
    <ProcessStep
      number={6}
      title="Keep a certified copy"
      description="After recording, request a certified copy of the deed from the clerk's office. Keep the recorded or certified copy with the divorce judgment and related property records."
      isLast={true}
    />
  </div>

  <!-- Consistency with Judgment -->
  <h2>Consistency with the Divorce Judgment</h2>
  <p>
    Do not prepare vesting, consideration, or ownership percentages that conflict with the final judgment,
    marital settlement agreement, or court order. The deed should implement what the court ordered &mdash;
    not restate or reinterpret it.
  </p>

  <CitableAnswerBlock
    question="Does a Florida divorce decree transfer property automatically?"
    answer="No. A Florida divorce decree orders the transfer but does not execute it. A quitclaim deed must be separately prepared, signed before two subscribing witnesses and a notary (Fla. Stat. §689.01), and recorded with the Clerk of Circuit Court in the county where the property is located. Failure to record leaves both spouses' names on the public title record regardless of what the court ordered."
    source="Fla. Stat. §689.01; Fla. Stat. §695.03; Fla. Stat. §695.01 (Last Verified: August 2026)"
  />

